.TH PMAP 9 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Developer's Manual"
.SH NAME
pmap \- machine-dependent virtual memory interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/arch/i386/pmap.h>
.LP
.BI "pmap_t pmap_create(void);"
.br
.BI "void pmap_destroy(pmap_t " pmap ");"
.br
.BI "void pmap_reference(pmap_t " pmap ");"
.br
.BI "void pmap_release(pmap_t " pmap ");"
.br
.BI "pmap_t pmap_fork(pmap_t " src ");"
.br
.BI "void pmap_activate(pmap_t " pmap ");"
.br
.BI "int pmap_enter(pmap_t " pmap ", uint32_t " va ", uint32_t " pa ", uint32_t " prot ", uint32_t " flags ");"
.br
.BI "int pmap_enter_pse(pmap_t " pmap ", uint32_t " va ", uint32_t " pa ", uint32_t " flags ");"
.br
.BI "void pmap_remove(pmap_t " pmap ", uint32_t " va ");"
.br
.BI "void pmap_protect(pmap_t " pmap ", uint32_t " sva ", uint32_t " eva ", uint32_t " prot ");"
.br
.BI "int pmap_copy(pmap_t " dst ", pmap_t " src ", uint32_t " sva ", uint32_t " eva ", int " cow ");"
.br
.BI "void pmap_kenter(uint32_t " va ", uint32_t " pa ");"
.br
.BI "void pmap_kremove(uint32_t " va ");"
.br
.BI "void pmap_dump(pmap_t " pmap ");"
.br
.BI "int pmap_check(pmap_t " pmap ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B pmap
module provides the machine-dependent interface to the virtual memory system. It manages per-process page tables and provides operations for mapping, unmapping, and protecting virtual address ranges.
.SS Address Space Management
.B pmap_create
allocates a new address space with an empty user region and shared kernel mappings.
.PP
.B pmap_destroy
frees all user page tables and physical pages, then releases the pmap structure.
.PP
.B pmap_fork
creates a copy of the source address space using Copy-on-Write (COW). Both parent and child share physical pages until a write occurs.
.PP
.B pmap_activate
loads the pmap's page directory into CR3, making it the current address space.
.SS Mapping Operations
.B pmap_enter
maps a virtual address
.I va
to physical address
.I pa
with the specified protection and flags.
.PP
.B pmap_enter_pse
creates a 4MB Page Size Extension (PSE) mapping from 4MB-aligned
.I va
to
.IR pa .
This uses a single Page Directory Entry (PDE) to map a large page, reducing TLB pressure.
.PP
.B pmap_remove
unmaps the virtual address
.IR va .
.PP
.B pmap_protect
changes the protection bits for a range of virtual addresses.
.PP
.B pmap_kenter
enters a mapping into the kernel address space. This creates a direct mapping from virtual address
.I va
to physical address
.IR pa .
It is intended for mapping kernel data structures and devices.
.PP
.B pmap_kremove
removes a mapping from the kernel address space at
.IR va .
.PP
.B pmap_copy
copies mappings from source to destination pmap. If
.I cow
is set, pages are marked read-only for copy-on-write.
.SS Page Tracking
.BI "int pmap_is_referenced(vm_page_t *" m ");
.br
.BI "void pmap_clear_reference(vm_page_t *" m ");
.br
.BI "int pmap_is_referenced_range(pmap_t " pmap ", uint32_t " sva ", uint32_t " eva ");
.br
.BI "int pmap_test_and_clear_ref(pmap_t " pmap ", uint32_t " va ");
.br
.BI "void pmap_track_access(vm_page_t *" m ");
.br
.BI "int pmap_is_modified_range(pmap_t " pmap ", uint32_t " sva ", uint32_t " eva ");
.br
.BI "int pmap_test_and_clear_modify(pmap_t " pmap ", uint32_t " va ");
.br
.BI "void pmap_track_modify(vm_page_t *" m ", uint32_t " current_time ");
.PP
These functions query and clear the hardware Accessed (A) and Dirty (D) bits in page table entries, used by page replacement algorithms.
.PP
.B pmap_is_referenced_range
counts accessed pages in a range.
.B pmap_test_and_clear_ref
atomically tests and clears the A bit.
.B pmap_track_access
updates per-page access frequency for aging.
.PP
.B pmap_is_modified_range
counts dirty pages in a range.
.B pmap_test_and_clear_modify
atomically tests and clears the D bit.
.B pmap_track_modify
records modification time for writeback scheduling.
.SH IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
.IP \[bu] 2
Uses recursive paging (PDE 1023 points to itself) for O(1) page table access.
.IP \[bu]
Kernel PDEs (768-1022) are shared by reference across all pmaps.
.IP \[bu]
TLB invalidation via INVLPG; batch invalidation for large ranges.
.IP \[bu]
Global pages (PGE): kernel pages marked with PG_G survive CR3 reloads.
Use
.B pmap_flush_global_pages
to flush global pages (toggles CR4.PGE).
.IP \[bu]
Copy-on-Write (COW) faults use direct kernel mapping for efficient memory copying, avoiding temporary mappings.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pmm (9),
.BR vm_page (9)
